---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { href } from "../../lib/site";
const toc = [
  { id: "cli", text: "With the CLI" },
  { id: "react", text: "React" },
  { id: "vue", text: "Vue" },
  { id: "svelte", text: "Svelte" },
  { id: "html", text: "Plain HTML" },
  { id: "own", text: "You own the code" },
];
---
<Docs title="Installation" seoTitle="Install VinUI in React, Vue, Svelte or HTML · VinUI" description="Add the free VinUI component library to a React, Vue, Svelte or plain HTML project in a minute, with one CLI command or a stylesheet link." current="installation" toc={toc}>
  <div class="crumbs"><a href={href("/")}>VinUI</a><span>Installation</span></div>
  <h1 class="page-title">Installation</h1>
  <p class="page-lead">Pick your style and accent in the header first. The commands on this site then use them.</p>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="cli">With the CLI</h2>
    <p>For React, Vue and Svelte. Run this in your project folder. It reads your framework from <code>package.json</code>, asks for a style, an accent colour and a folder, then writes <code>vin.json</code> and <code>vin.css</code>. It also puts <code>data-vin-style</code> on your <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> tag when it can find it (<code>index.html</code>, <code>app/layout.tsx</code>, <code>src/app.html</code> or <code>app.vue</code>); if it can't, it tells you what to add.</p>
    <CodeBlock title="Terminal" lang="bash" code="npx @vinish/ui init" />
    <p>Then add components. Each one is copied into your project, with any component it needs.</p>
    <CodeBlock title="Terminal" lang="bash" code="npx @vinish/ui add button card dialog" />
    <p>What's left is importing <code>vin.css</code> once and using the components, which differs a little per framework.</p>

    <h2 id="react">React</h2>
    <p>React 18 or newer. Works with Vite, Next.js (App Router or Pages) and React Router. Components go in <code>src/components/vin</code>. The interactive ones start with <code>"use client"</code>, so they work in Server Components pages.</p>
    <CodeBlock title="src/main.tsx (Vite) or app/layout.tsx (Next.js)" lang="tsx" code={`import "./vin.css";`} />
    <CodeBlock title="App.tsx" lang="tsx" code={`import { Button } from "@/components/vin/button";\n\nexport function App() {\n  return <Button>Save</Button>;\n}`} />

    <h2 id="vue">Vue</h2>
    <p>Vue 3.4 or newer, as single-file components (<code>.vue</code>). Works with Vite and Nuxt. Components go in <code>src/components/vin</code>.</p>
    <CodeBlock title="src/main.ts (Vite) or nuxt.config.ts css: [...] (Nuxt)" lang="ts" code={`import "./vin.css";`} />
    <CodeBlock title="App.vue" lang="vue" code={`<script setup lang="ts">\nimport Button from "@/components/vin/button.vue";\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <Button>Save</Button>\n</template>`} />
    <p>A component with parts exports them from the same file: <code>import Card, &#123; CardHeader, CardTitle &#125; from "@/components/vin/card.vue"</code>.</p>

    <h2 id="svelte">Svelte</h2>
    <p>Svelte 5.29 or newer (runes). Works with SvelteKit and Vite. In SvelteKit, components go in <code>src/lib/components/vin</code>, so you import them from <code>$lib</code>.</p>
    <CodeBlock title="src/routes/+layout.svelte" lang="svelte" code={`<script>\n  import "../vin.css";\n  let { children } = $props();\n</script>\n\n{@render children()}`} />
    <CodeBlock title="src/routes/+page.svelte" lang="svelte" code={`<script lang="ts">\n  import Button from "$lib/components/vin/button.svelte";\n</script>\n\n<Button>Save</Button>`} />
    <p>Svelte has one component per file, so parts have their own files: <code>card-header.svelte</code>, <code>card-title.svelte</code>. <code>add card</code> copies them all.</p>

    <p>Vue and Svelte use <code>v-model</code> and <code>bind:</code>, slots and snippets where React uses props. <a href={href("/docs/frameworks/")}>Frameworks</a> lists the differences.</p>

    <h2 id="html">Plain HTML</h2>
    <p>No build step and no CLI. Link one stylesheet for your style, set the style on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, and copy any component's HTML from its page.</p>
    <CodeBlock title="index.html" lang="html" code={`<html data-vin-style="precision" style="--vin-accent: #5b5bd6">\n  <head>\n    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://vinish.dev/ui/v1/precision.css">\n    <!-- Only for interactive components (Select, Tabs, Dropdown Menu…) -->\n    <script src="https://vinish.dev/ui/v1/vinui.js" defer></script>\n  </head>`} />
    <p>Each component page says whether it needs <code>vinui.js</code>. Dialog, Accordion, Popover and others use the browser's own elements and work without it. <code>vinui.js</code> finds components when the page loads; for markup you add later, call <code>VinUI.init(element)</code>.</p>

    <h2 id="own">You own the code</h2>
    <p>Nothing runs from our servers. Edit the files as much as you like; <code>npx @vinish/ui diff</code> shows what changed in VinUI since you added a component, and <code>add --overwrite</code> takes the new version.</p>
    <p>Next: <a href={href("/docs/theming/")}>Theming</a>, <a href={href("/docs/frameworks/")}>Frameworks</a>, <a href={href("/docs/cli/")}>the CLI</a> and <a href={href("/docs/ai/")}>AI tools</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Docs>
